      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, you can pressure wash a wood deck as long as you keep the pressure low, typically around 500 to 1,200 PSI for softwoods like pressure-treated pine and cedar, use a 25 or 40 degree tip held roughly 10 to 12 inches from the surface, and move steadily with the grain. Too much pressure, too narrow a tip or holding the spray in one place gouges the wood and raises fibers, a problem called furring that has to be sanded out before you can stain. Composite decking can usually be pressure washed too, but only within the limits in the manufacturer's care guide, which vary by brand. The best results come from letting a deck cleaner do the chemical work first and using the washer only to rinse, then letting the wood dry thoroughly before staining or sealing.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is Pressure Washing Bad for a Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pressure washing is not bad for a deck. Pressure washing badly is. The reputation comes from people using a gas machine at full power with a narrow tip, holding it close to soft lumber and carving grooves into the boards. Done with low pressure, a wide fan and a steady motion, a pressure washer is one of the fastest ways to rinse a deck clean, and it is what most professional deck restorers use as part of their prep.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key idea is that wood is soft and has a grain. The lighter spring growth in each growth ring is much softer than the darker late growth, and high-pressure water erodes the soft layers faster, leaving a rough, ridged surface. Pine, cedar and redwood, the most common deck woods, are all softwoods and easy to damage. Even dense hardwoods can be marked if you hold the spray too close for too long.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other key idea is that pressure alone does not clean a deck very well. It removes loose dirt, but it does not reliably kill mildew or lift the gray, sun-damaged surface layer that makes old wood look tired. That is the job of a deck cleaner, usually an oxygen-based product, which loosens grime and graying so that a gentle rinse can carry it away. When people turn up the pressure to compensate for skipping the cleaner, that is when the damage happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the realistic answer is yes, with conditions. Use a cleaner, keep the pressure low, use a wide tip at a safe distance, follow the grain, and test in an inconspicuous spot first. If you do those things, a pressure washer saves hours over hand scrubbing and leaves the wood ready for stain. If you would rather not risk it, a garden hose, a stiff brush and a good deck cleaner can get you most of the way there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="psi" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What PSI Should You Use to Pressure Wash a Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most softwood decks, start around 500 to 600 PSI and work up only as needed, staying at or below about 1,200 PSI. That range covers pressure-treated pine, cedar and redwood, which together make up most residential decks. Cedar and redwood are particularly soft, so stay toward the bottom of the range. Pressure-treated pine is a little more forgiving but still marks easily when wet.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Decking</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical pressure guidance</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tip</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cedar, redwood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 500 to 800 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 degree</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very soft, start low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure-treated pine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 500 to 1,200 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 or 40 degree</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most common deck wood</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tropical hardwood (ipe, etc.)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to about 1,500 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 or 40 degree</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dense, needs little pressure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Composite or PVC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per manufacturer care guide</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fan tip only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limits vary by brand</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Hardwoods like ipe, cumaru and other tropical species are much denser and can tolerate more, typically up to around 1,500 PSI. They rarely need it, though. The surface of a hardwood deck is not absorbent, and most of the grime and graying sits on top, so a cleaner and a modest rinse usually do the job. Using more pressure than you need on a premium hardwood deck has no upside.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most pressure washers do not let you dial in an exact pressure. Instead you control effective pressure at the surface with the nozzle and the distance. A wider tip spreads the force over a larger area, and moving back from the surface reduces impact quickly. That is why a 2,000 PSI electric machine or even a 3,000 PSI gas machine can be used safely on a deck if you choose the right tip and keep your distance. If yours has an adjustable pressure setting, use the lowest one first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric machines are often the better fit for decks for exactly this reason: their lower maximum pressure leaves less room for mistakes. Our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washers</a> guide covers models that clean decks well without the power that makes gas machines risky on soft wood. If you are weighing whether to buy or rent for one deck, our guide on <a href="/how-much-does-a-pressure-washer-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much a pressure washer costs</a> runs the numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Tip, Distance and Technique Should You Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a 25 degree tip, usually green, or a 40 degree tip, usually white. Never use a 0 degree red tip or 15 degree yellow tip on a deck; they concentrate the spray into a narrow jet that cuts into wood almost immediately. If you are unsure which color is which on your machine, the <a href="/pressure-washer-nozzle-colors-explained/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washer nozzle colors</a> guide explains the standard system. A rotating turbo nozzle is also a bad idea on wood, because it combines a narrow jet with movement that can etch circles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hold the tip roughly 10 to 12 inches from the surface to start, and only move closer if the grime is not coming off. Start your spray before the tip reaches the board, and keep it moving the whole time. Stopping, even briefly, lets the stream dwell on one spot and can leave a visible mark. Many experienced deck cleaners begin each stroke off the deck or at the house wall and sweep across in one continuous pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always work with the grain, along the length of the boards, never across them. Spraying across the grain catches the softer wood between growth rings and tears it up much faster. Overlap each pass slightly so you do not leave stripes, and work one or two boards at a time from one end of the deck to the other. Clean the boards end to end in a single pass where you can so you do not create lap marks in the middle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test first, then commit</h3>

          <p>
            Before touching the main floor, test on a hidden area such as a board under the stairs, behind a planter or at the edge where it will not show. Start at the widest tip and the farthest distance and move in gradually until the dirt lifts. Let the test area dry and check it with your hand. If it feels fuzzy or rough, you are too close or using too much pressure, and you should back off before cleaning the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="furring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Furring and How Do You Fix It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Furring is the fuzzy, splintery surface that appears when high-pressure water raises and tears wood fibers. It often looks fine while the deck is wet and only shows up once it dries, when the boards feel hairy to the touch and look lighter and rougher. Furring is the most common sign that a deck was washed with too much pressure or too close a tip. It is especially common on cedar and pine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Furring is a problem mainly because of what comes next. Stain soaks unevenly into raised fibers, giving a blotchy finish, and the loose fibers break off over time, taking stain with them. A furred deck that is stained without sanding often looks worse after a year than before it was cleaned. Furring also makes the deck feel rough underfoot and catches dirt more easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is sanding once the wood is fully dry. A light pass with a pole sander or orbital sander using roughly 60 to 80 grit paper removes the raised fibers without closing up the wood's pores so tightly that stain cannot penetrate. Sweep or blow off the dust afterward. Our guide on <a href="/do-you-need-to-sand-a-deck-before-staining/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether you need to sand a deck before staining</a> explains when sanding is necessary and when it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gouges and lap marks are harder to fix than furring. Deep grooves cut by a narrow tip often need heavier sanding or, in severe cases, flipping or replacing the board. Lighter lap marks, the stripes left by uneven passes, usually blend in after sanding and staining. The best defense is prevention: wide tip, low pressure, steady motion, and a test patch before you start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="composite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Can You Pressure Wash a Composite Deck?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually, yes, but within limits that depend on the brand and product line. Composite and PVC decking manufacturers publish care guides that specify whether pressure washing is allowed, the maximum pressure, the nozzle type and the minimum distance. Some allow a fan tip at moderate pressure; others recommend a garden hose and soft brush only for routine cleaning. Following those rules matters, because damage from pressure washing beyond the stated limits may not be covered by the warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            Composite boards are not damaged the same way wood is, but they can be. High pressure can erode the protective cap layer on capped composites or roughen the embossed surface on older uncapped boards. That damage is permanent because there is no grain to sand back and refinishing is not recommended by most manufacturers. Once the surface is roughened, it holds dirt and mold spores more easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most composite decks, a soft-bristle brush and a cleaner approved by the decking manufacturer do most of the work, with a garden hose or a gentle fan-tip rinse to finish. Mold and mildew spots on composite are usually surface growth feeding on pollen and debris in the embossed texture rather than damage to the board, and a suitable cleaner removes them more effectively than pressure. Do not use a wire brush or abrasive pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are planning to change the look of a composite deck, cleaning is only part of the story. Most composite boards are not designed to be stained, and our guide on <a href="/can-you-stain-composite-decking/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether you can stain composite decking</a> covers the few cases where it works and the many where it does not. For cleaning alone, the manufacturer's care guide is the authority, and it is worth reading before you plug in a washer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do You Need a Deck Cleaner Before Pressure Washing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For a good result, yes. An oxygen-based deck cleaner, usually sodium percarbonate, breaks down mildew, algae and the gray oxidized layer on old wood so that a low-pressure rinse can carry it away. Apply it to damp wood with a pump sprayer or roller, let it dwell for the time on the label, typically around 10 to 15 minutes without letting it dry, and scrub stubborn areas with a stiff synthetic brush before rinsing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid chlorine bleach on wood decks. It can kill surface mildew, but it does little for the dirt and graying, it can leave wood looking unnaturally blotchy or washed out, it is hard on plants and metal fasteners, and residue can interfere with stain. If you are considering it as a shortcut, our guide on <a href="/can-you-use-bleach-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">using bleach in a pressure washer</a> explains why most manufacturers say no to running it through the machine as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            After cleaning, many deck restorers apply a brightener, usually based on oxalic or citric acid. Cleaners are alkaline and can darken wood slightly; brighteners neutralize that, restore the wood's color and remove some tannin stains and rust marks from fasteners. Our <a href="/deck-brightener-vs-deck-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">deck brightener vs deck cleaner</a> guide explains when you need both and when the cleaner alone is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect your plants and surroundings throughout. Wet nearby plants and grass before applying any cleaner, cover delicate plants with a breathable sheet if you can, and rinse them again afterward. Keep runoff out of storm drains, and follow the label for any product you use. The <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to clean a deck</a> guide walks through the full process step by step, and our <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best deck cleaner</a> roundup covers which products work best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long Should a Deck Dry Before Staining?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Typically at least 48 hours of dry weather, and longer in cool, humid or shaded conditions. Many stain manufacturers specify a minimum dry time after washing, and some recommend checking moisture content directly with a moisture meter. Pressure washing pushes water deep into the wood, and stain applied to wet wood does not penetrate properly, which leads to peeling and uneven color.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple field test is the water drop test. Sprinkle a few drops of water on the deck. If they soak in within a minute or so, the wood is dry and open enough to accept stain. If they bead up or sit on the surface, the wood is still too wet or has old finish on it that needs more prep. Check several boards, including shaded areas and those near the house, which dry more slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the drying time to inspect. Look for furring that needs sanding, popped nails or screws, loose boards and areas where old stain is still peeling. This is the time to sand, reset fasteners and replace damaged boards, since all of that is harder after staining. Our <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to prep a deck for staining</a> guide covers the full checklist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather matters for staining too. Most stains need a dry window after application as well as before, and many labels specify a temperature range and no rain for at least a day. If the forecast is uncertain, it is better to wait a few more days than to rush. A well-cleaned, fully dry deck takes stain evenly and holds it much longer than one stained in a hurry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about how often you really need to do this. A full pressure wash and restain every year is more than most decks need, and every wash removes a little wood. Light cleaning with a hose and soft brush between full restorations keeps a deck looking good with much less wear. Save the pressure washer for when the deck genuinely needs it, and use it gently when you do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Narrow Tip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 0 and 15 degree tips concentrate the spray into a jet that cuts grooves into soft wood in seconds. Use a 25 or 40 degree tip on any deck, and never a turbo or rotating nozzle. If you need more cleaning power, use a better cleaner or a brush, not a narrower spray.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Across the Grain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spraying across the boards catches the soft wood between growth rings and tears it, leaving a rough, ridged surface. Always move the spray along the length of the boards, overlap each pass slightly, and keep the wand moving so it never dwells on one spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Cleaner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pressure alone removes loose dirt but not mildew or graying, so people turn up the pressure to compensate and damage the wood. An oxygen-based deck cleaner does the chemical work, and a low-pressure rinse carries it away. The result is cleaner wood with far less risk.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Composite Care Guide</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Composite and PVC decking manufacturers specify whether you can pressure wash, the maximum pressure, the tip and the distance. Going beyond those limits can permanently damage the surface and may affect the warranty. Read the care guide for your specific brand and product line before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pressure washing drives water deep into the wood, and stain applied to damp boards does not penetrate properly, leading to peeling and blotchy color. Wait for at least two dry days, longer in cool or humid weather, and use the water drop test before you open the can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What PSI is safe for a pressure-treated deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically about 500 to 1,200 PSI, starting at the low end and working up only if needed. Use a 25 or 40 degree tip, hold it roughly 10 to 12 inches away and keep it moving with the grain. Test on a hidden board first and let it dry to check for fuzziness before cleaning the rest.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a gas pressure washer on a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you control the pressure at the surface. Use the widest tip, start well back from the boards and move closer only as needed. Many gas machines produce far more pressure than wood needs, so they leave less margin for error. An electric machine is often easier to use safely on a deck.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will pressure washing remove deck stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can loosen and remove some failing or peeling stain, but it is not a reliable stripping method and using enough pressure to remove sound stain usually damages the wood. For stain removal, use a deck stripper made for the type of finish you have, then rinse at low pressure and neutralize with a brightener.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to sand after pressure washing a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the wood is furred, rough or uneven after it dries. Run your hand over several boards. If they feel fuzzy, a light pass with roughly 60 to 80 grit paper removes the raised fibers. If the surface is smooth and the water drop test shows it absorbs, you can usually stain without sanding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you pressure wash a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most decks only need a full pressure wash when they are being prepared for a new coat of stain, which is typically every few years depending on the product and exposure. In between, rinse with a hose and scrub with a soft brush and mild cleaner. Every pressure wash removes a little wood, so do it only when needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash a deck without a cleaner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but the results are usually worse and the risk is higher. Pressure alone removes loose dirt but not mildew spores or the gray oxidized layer, so people tend to use more pressure to compensate. An oxygen-based deck cleaner does the real cleaning, and the washer only needs to rinse it away.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can pressure washing damage the house next to the deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Spraying upward under siding can force water behind it, and high pressure can damage window seals, screens, wiring and outdoor lights. Keep the spray pointed down at the deck surface, work away from the house, cover outlets and fixtures, and keep the wand well back from doors and windows.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

