        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            You can pressure wash a deck safely, and it is often the fastest way to get one ready for stain, as long as you treat the washer as a rinse tool. Keep softwood decks around 500 to 1,200 PSI, use a 25 or 40 degree tip at roughly 10 to 12 inches, move with the grain and test first. Let a deck cleaner do the actual cleaning.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For composite decking, follow the manufacturer's care guide exactly, because limits vary and damage is permanent. For wood, sand out any furring, let the deck dry for at least a couple of days, and use the water drop test before you stain. Done that way, a pressure washer makes deck care easier rather than harder.
          </p>
